::GEO_U01_PT_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
To copy segment AB with a compass and straightedge, which action should happen first?
{
~A. Estimate the length of AB by sight#This relies on appearance instead of construction evidence. Copying a segment requires preserving the exact compass opening from the original segment.
=B. Set the compass opening to the length of AB#Correct. The compass opening transfers the length of AB so the copied segment can be congruent to the original.
~C. Draw any segment that looks close to AB#This creates an approximation, not a construction. A copied segment must use the original segment length as evidence.
~D. Measure AB with a protractor#A protractor measures angles, not segment length. Segment copying uses a compass and straightedge.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
If angle DEF is constructed as a copy of angle ABC, which statement should be true?
{
~A. mangle DEF = 90°#This assumes the copied angle is right. A copied angle has the same measure as the original angle, whatever that measure is.
~B. DE = AB#This compares segment lengths, not angle measures. Copying an angle preserves the angle measure.
~C. angle DEF is larger than angle ABC#This describes a failed copy. A copied angle should match the original angle measure.
=D. mangle DEF = mangle ABC#Correct. A copied angle is congruent to the original angle, so their measures are equal.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
If M is the midpoint of segment AB, which statement must be true?
{
=A. AM = MB#Correct. A midpoint divides a segment into two equal lengths.
~B. AM + MB = 90°#This mixes segment length with angle measure. Midpoints divide segments into two congruent parts.
~C. AB = AM#This would make M the same distance as the full segment length, not the midpoint. The full segment is made of AM and MB.
~D. M is outside segment AB#A midpoint lies on the segment it divides. A point outside AB cannot be the midpoint of AB.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.2
Question:
Which description matches a perpendicular bisector of segment AB?
{
~A. A line that passes through A and B#This is the line containing the segment, not a perpendicular bisector. A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at its midpoint.
~B. A ray that creates two angles of any measures#This describes neither perpendicular nor bisecting behavior. Both right-angle intersection and midpoint division are required.
=C. A line that crosses AB at its midpoint and forms right angles#Correct. A perpendicular bisector both bisects the segment and is perpendicular to it.
~D. A segment that is longer than AB#Length alone does not define a perpendicular bisector. The key evidence is midpoint and right angles.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If angle 1 and angle 2 form a linear pair, which relationship is guaranteed?
{
~A. mangle 1 = mangle 2#A linear pair does not require equal angle measures. Equal measures would only happen in special cases.
=B. mangle 1 + mangle 2 = 180°#Correct. Angles that form a linear pair are supplementary, so their measures add to 180°.
~C. mangle 1 + mangle 2 = 90°#This describes complementary angles, not a linear pair. A linear pair forms a straight angle.
~D. angle 1 and angle 2 are always vertical angles#Linear pairs are adjacent angles. Vertical angles are opposite angles formed by intersecting lines.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.1
Question:
If two angles are vertical angles, which conclusion is valid?
{
=A. The angles are congruent#Correct. Vertical angles formed by intersecting lines are congruent.
~B. The angles form a linear pair#Vertical angles are opposite, not adjacent. A linear pair must be adjacent and supplementary.
~C. The angle measures add to 90°#Vertical angles are congruent, but their sum is not necessarily 90°.
~D. Each angle measures 180°#A vertical angle can have many possible measures. Congruence is the guaranteed relationship.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
For the statement If a figure is a square, then it has four right angles, which part is the hypothesis?
{
~A. It has four right angles#This is the conclusion, or the result after then. The hypothesis follows if.
~B. The figure is not a square#This negates the hypothesis. It belongs to related statements such as the inverse or contrapositive.
~C. It does not have four right angles#This negates the conclusion. It is not the hypothesis of the original statement.
=D. A figure is a square#Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the converse of If p, then q?
{
~A. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~B. If not q, then not p#This is the contrapositive. It switches and negates both parts.
=C. If q, then p#Correct. The converse switches the order of the original conditional.
~D. p if and only if q#This is a biconditional statement. It combines a conditional and its converse when both are true.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.5
Question:
In a geometric proof, why are reasons included with statements?
{
=A. To justify each step using definitions, properties, postulates, or theorems#Correct. Reasons show why each statement follows logically.
~B. To make the proof longer#Length is not the purpose of proof. Reasons justify why each statement is valid.
~C. To replace diagrams entirely#Diagrams can support reasoning, but reasons explain the logical support for each step.
~D. To show that a conclusion looks true#Appearance is not proof. A valid proof needs logical support for each conclusion.
}
::GEO_U01_PT_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U01_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.6
Question:
Which counterexample disproves the claim All quadrilaterals are rectangles?
{
~A. A square#A square is a rectangle, so it supports the claim for one case instead of disproving it.
~B. A rectangle with length 6 and width 4#This is still a rectangle, so it cannot disprove the claim.
~C. A rectangle with four right angles#This repeats the rectangle condition and does not provide a non-rectangle quadrilateral.
=D. A kite with no right angles#Correct. A kite can be a quadrilateral that is not a rectangle, so it disproves the universal claim.
}
